Task-Specific Sufficiency of Regenerative Positional Memory: 

A Task-Conditioned Information-Theoretic Framework with Architecture-Constrained Decoding and Regenerative Execution

ouadi Maakoul 

AI : chatGpt, Deepseek, Gemini 


Abstract

The regeneration of an amputated appendage requires that residual tissue know what it must rebuild and where. This thesis develops a rigorous information-theoretic framework for positional memory in regenerative systems, formalizing sufficiency as a property of the triplet (M_t, \Theta_{\mathcal A}, \varepsilon)—memory representation, architecture class, and task tolerance—rather than of memory content alone.

Positional memory is modeled as a discrete-time stochastic process M_t decoded by an architecture-constrained stochastic kernel q_\theta(\hat c \mid m) parameterized by \theta = (\theta_{\rm ch}, \theta_{\rm fb}, \theta_{\rm cell}) \in \Theta_{\mathcal A} \subset \mathbb{R}^p, where \theta_{\rm ch} collects signaling channel parameters, \theta_{\rm fb} collects feedback-gain parameters, and \theta_{\rm cell} collects cellular competence parameters. Sufficiency is evaluated relative to a task-defined partition \phi: X \to C of anatomical positions into K regenerative classes.

The framework introduces four central quantities: the task-relevant memory information I(C; M_t); the architecture-constrained class error D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}(t) = \inf_{\theta \in \Theta_{\mathcal A}} P_e(q_\theta; M_t); the task rate-distortion threshold R_C(\varepsilon) = \inf_{P_{\hat C \mid C}:\, P_e \le \varepsilon} I(C; \hat C); and the regenerative rate-distortion function R_{\rm regen}(\varepsilon) = \inf_{P_{A \mid C}:\, \mathbb E[L(C,A,Y)] \le \varepsilon} I(C; A), which constrains the full chain from positional class to regenerative action.

Two impossibility theorems are established. First, at the class level: if I(C; M_t) < R_C(\varepsilon), then no decoder—including any architecture-constrained decoder—can achieve error \le \varepsilon. The converse does not hold: sufficient memory information does not guarantee that the biological architecture can extract it. Second, at the execution level: even perfect class decoding does not guarantee regenerative success, since D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}(t) \le \varepsilon_C \not\Rightarrow D_{\mathcal A}^{Y,*}(t) \le \varepsilon_Y. Together these yield two formally distinct and experimentally separable bottlenecks: an information bottleneck and an architecture/execution bottleneck.

The framework corrects the asymptotic behavior of the generalized Fano bound for non-uniform priors, establishing that P_e^{\rm chance} = 1 - \max_i p(c_i) rather than 1 - 1/K. It replaces naive outcome coding with a task-conditioned regenerative rate-distortion formulation. It formalizes decoder–memory interaction as a closed-loop active memory system M_{t+1} \sim P_M(\cdot \mid M_t, \hat C_t, U_t) with exogenous input U_t, and proves that decoder-only write-back cannot increase I(C; M_t) without an information-bearing exogenous source. It defines persistent retention time \tau_{\rm pers}(\varepsilon) to account for active information recovery, distinguishing it from first-loss time \tau_{\downarrow}(\varepsilon). It establishes functional identifiability \theta \sim_{\rm func} \theta' \iff D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}(\theta) = D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}(\theta'), ensuring that architecture performance is estimable without requiring identification of every biological parameter. It provides a validated Blahut–Arimoto computation of R_C(\varepsilon) for uniform and non-uniform priors, with corrected numerical values satisfying all analytical boundary conditions. It specifies a hierarchical Bayesian estimator for I(C; M_t) derived from the posterior predictive distribution rather than a Gaussian surrogate for mutual information. It replaces analytic sample-size formulas with simulation-based assurance for equivalence testing.

The framework yields three refutable predictions for the axolotl, killifish, and planarian systems, each with pre-registered equivalence margins \delta_I = \eta_I \cdot R_C(\varepsilon) and \delta_D = \eta_D \cdot \varepsilon, sensitivity analysis over \eta_I, \eta_D \in \{0.05, 0.10, 0.20\}, and explicit falsification criteria. A state-dependent architecture hypothesis \Theta_{\mathcal A}^{\rm mature} \neq \Theta_{\mathcal A}^{\rm blastema} is proposed for the axolotl, motivated by evidence that positional memory reprogrammability is itself state-dependent. The distinction between measured and biological memory, M_{\rm measured} \neq M_{\rm biological}, is made explicit throughout.

The central thesis is that task-specific sufficiency of regenerative positional memory is a property of (M_t, \Theta_{\mathcal A}, \varepsilon), not of M_t alone. The framework provides a formal language in which the sufficiency of positional memory can be quantified, bounded, and experimentally interrogated, yielding a rigorous hierarchy from position to task to memory to decoder to action to regenerative phenotype.


1. Introduction

1.1 The Central Question

The regeneration of an amputated appendage requires that residual tissue know what it must rebuild and where. Since Wolpert's formulation of positional information, the morphogenetic field has been understood as a spatial coordinate system encoded in molecular signals. The question addressed here is narrower and more precise:

How much information about anatomical position must be preserved in residual tissue for the available biological architecture to execute a successful regenerative program?

This question is not mechanistic. It is informational and architectural. It asks for necessary conditions, achievable bounds, and the formal distinction between failures caused by insufficient memory and failures caused by insufficient decoding capacity.

1.2 Central Thesis

The central claim of this work is:

\boxed{
\text{Task-specific sufficiency of regenerative positional memory is a property of the triplet }
(M_t, \Theta_{\mathcal A}, \varepsilon),
\text{ not of } M_t \text{ alone.}
}

Positional memory may contain abundant information about anatomical position and still fail to support regeneration if the available biological architecture cannot extract that information. Conversely, a sufficiently capable architecture may compensate for degraded memory. Sufficiency is therefore a relational property between memory content, architectural capacity, and task tolerance.

1.3 Scope

This work constructs a measurement and constraint framework. It does not propose a new molecular mechanism of regeneration. It provides a formal language in which the sufficiency of positional memory can be quantified, bounded, and experimentally interrogated. The framework interfaces with existing mechanistic models without replacing them.

