2. Fundamental Objects

2.1 Anatomical Space and Regenerative Classes

Let X \subset \mathbb{R}^d be the anatomical space of positions with reference measure \mu_X. For a vertebrate limb, d = 3. Let

\phi : X \to C = \{c_1, c_2, \dots, c_K\}

be a partition of anatomical positions into K regenerative classes. Two positions x_a, x_b \in X belong to the same class if and only if

\phi(x_a) = \phi(x_b) \iff R(x_a) \sim R(x_b),

where R denotes the regenerative response. This many-to-one mapping is an information bottleneck: the tissue may discard fine-grained positional details irrelevant to qualitative program execution. The partition \phi is task-defined and constitutes an experimental design choice.

2.2 Measurable Biological State and Memory Representations

Let Z_t denote the raw molecular and physiological state of the tissue at time t, comprising chromatin accessibility, transcription factor occupancy, transcriptomic profiles, bioelectric potentials, and extracellular matrix composition.

Positional memory is not assumed to be a single molecular object. We define a family of candidate memory representations:

M_t^{(j)} = f_j(Z_t), \qquad j \in \mathcal J = \{\text{chromatin}, \text{TF}, \text{transcriptomic}, \text{bioelectric}, \text{ECM}, \text{multimodal}\}.

Each f_j is a specified, pre-registered feature map. The default representation M_t is the multimodal concatenation, with axis-specific analyses conducted on each M_t^{(j)}.

2.3 Parameterized Architecture Class

The admissible decoder set is parameterized by a finite-dimensional vector

\theta = (\theta_{\rm ch}, \theta_{\rm fb}, \theta_{\rm cell}) \in \Theta_{\mathcal A} \subset \mathbb{R}^p,

where:

· \theta_{\rm ch} collects signaling channel parameters: diffusion coefficients, degradation rates, effective channel capacities for morphogen-mediated communication.
· \theta_{\rm fb} collects feedback-gain parameters: Hand2–Shh loop gain, gap-junction conductance, auto-regulatory strengths.
· \theta_{\rm cell} collects cellular competence parameters: density of competent cells, receptor availability, neoblast recruitment rates.

For each \theta \in \Theta_{\mathcal A}, the decoder is a stochastic kernel

q_\theta(\hat c \mid m) = \mathbb P(\hat C_t = \hat c \mid M_t = m; \theta).

The optimal architecture-constrained class error is

\boxed{\,D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}(t) \;=\; \inf_{\theta \in \Theta_{\mathcal A}} P_e(q_\theta; M_t)\,.}

Identifiability requirement. The parameterization renders D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}(t) empirically estimable only under an identifiable or functionally identifiable parameterization. Define functional identifiability:

\theta \sim_{\rm func} \theta'
\iff
D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}(\theta) = D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}(\theta').

The framework requires only identification of the equivalence class determining decoding performance, not estimation of every biological parameter individually.

2.4 Regenerative Output and the Full Generative Chain

Let Y_{t+\Delta} denote the anatomical or functional regenerative outcome at time t+\Delta. The full generative chain is

X \;\rightarrow\; C \;\rightarrow\; M_t \;\rightarrow\; \hat C_t \;\rightarrow\; A_t \;\rightarrow\; Y_{t+\Delta},

where A_t denotes the downstream regenerative action or signaling program. The framework analyzes the full chain, treating C \to M_t \to \hat C_t as an intermediate decoding stage and Y_{t+\Delta} as the ultimate performance criterion.

