3. Task-Relevant Information and Representation

3.1 Deterministic Decomposition

Since C = \phi(X) is deterministic, the chain X \to C \to M_t is Markov, and the data-processing inequality gives

I(C; M_t) \le I(X; M_t).

This decomposition is exact:

I(X; M_t) = I(C; M_t) + I(X; M_t \mid C).

We define:

Task-relevant positional information:

\boxed{\,I_{\rm task}(M_t) \;=\; I(C; M_t)\,}

Residual positional information:

\boxed{\,I_{\rm residual}(M_t) \;=\; I(X; M_t \mid C)\,}

The term I_{\rm residual} measures positional information not captured by the regenerative class. It can be interpreted as task-irrelevant only if C fully specifies the regenerative task. In general, residual positional detail may influence wound geometry, mechanical constraints, cell migration, and tissue boundary conditions, and therefore may not be strictly task-irrelevant.

3.2 Task-Efficiency Diagnostic

Define the task-efficiency ratio

\eta_{\rm task}(M_t) = \frac{I(C;M_t)}{I(X;M_t) + \epsilon},

where \epsilon > 0 is a small numerical regularizer. Because X is continuous and M_t may be high-dimensional, estimating I(X; M_t) may be substantially harder than estimating I(C; M_t). The ratio \eta_{\rm task} is therefore a secondary descriptive statistic. The primary empirical endpoint is I(C; M_t).

---

4. Information-Theoretic Bounds

4.1 Generalized Fano Bound

Let C have non-uniform prior p(c_i). Let P_e(t) = \mathbb P[\hat C_t \neq C] be the decoding error. The standard Fano inequality gives

H(C \mid M_t) \le h(P_e(t)) + P_e(t) \log_2(K-1),

where h(\cdot) is the binary entropy function. Since I(C; M_t) = H(C) - H(C \mid M_t),

\boxed{\,I(C; M_t) \;\ge\; H(C) - h(P_e(t)) - P_e(t)\log_2(K-1)\,.}

4.2 Asymptotic Chance-Level Correction

As t \to \infty under information loss, I(C; M_t) \to 0, and the optimal decoder selects the maximum-prior class:

P_e^{\rm chance} = 1 - \max_i p(c_i).

For uniform priors, P_e^{\rm chance} = 1 - 1/K. The non-uniform correction is essential for biological applications where regenerative classes may have unequal prior probabilities.

4.3 Task Rate-Distortion Function

Under Hamming distortion d_C(c, \hat c) = \mathbf 1[c \neq \hat c], define

\boxed{\,R_C(\varepsilon) \;=\; \inf_{P_{\hat C \mid C}\,:\, P_e \le \varepsilon} I(C; \hat C)\,.}

This is the minimum information that must be preserved about C to guarantee the existence of some decoder achieving error \le \varepsilon. It is a property of the source C and the distortion criterion alone; it is decoder-agnostic.

For uniform priors p(c_i) = 1/K,

R_C(D) = \log_2 K - h(D) - D \log_2(K-1), \qquad 0 \le D \le 1 - \frac{1}{K}.

For binary non-uniform priors p = (p, 1-p),

R_C(D) =
\begin{cases}
h(p) - h(D), & 0 \le D < \min(p, 1-p), \\
0, & D \ge \min(p, 1-p).
\end{cases}

---

5. Task-Conditioned Regenerative Rate-Distortion

5.1 From Class Decoding to Regenerative Outcome

The ultimate performance criterion is the regenerative outcome Y_{t+\Delta}, not the class label C. The biological system does not encode and reconstruct Y; it produces Y through the action of the architecture on the decoded class. The correct formulation is therefore task-conditioned:

\boxed{
R_{C \to Y}(\varepsilon)
=
\inf_{P_{\hat C \mid C}:\,
\mathbb E[d_Y(Y, \hat Y(\hat C))] \le \varepsilon}
I(C; \hat C).
}

Here \hat Y(\hat C) is the regenerative outcome induced by the decoded class \hat C, and d_Y is a distortion measure on outcomes—normalized limb-length discrepancy, skeletal-pattern mismatch, or functional score.

5.2 Biological Task Loss Formulation

More generally, define the biological task loss L(C, A, Y) and the regenerative rate-distortion function

\boxed{
R_{\rm regen}(\varepsilon)
=
\inf_{P_{A \mid C}:\,
\mathbb E[L(C, A, Y)] \le \varepsilon}
I(C; A).
}

Then

\boxed{
I(C; M_t) < R_{\rm regen}(\varepsilon)
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\text{no decoder operating on } M_t
\text{ can achieve the target regenerative performance.}
}

This is the correct information-theoretic constraint on regenerative action. It establishes the minimum positional information necessary for acceptable regeneration, conditional on the available architecture and the tolerance \varepsilon.

---

6. Impossibility Theorem and the Two Bottlenecks

6.1 Class-Level Impossibility

For any decoder q operating on M_t, the data-processing inequality gives

I(C; \hat C_q) \le I(C; M_t).

Therefore, if I(C; M_t) < R_C(\varepsilon), no decoder—including any architecture-constrained decoder—can achieve P_e \le \varepsilon:

\boxed{\,I(C; M_t) < R_C(\varepsilon) \;\;\Longrightarrow\;\; P_e(q) > \varepsilon \;\; \forall\, q \in \mathcal Q_{\mathcal A}\,.}

6.2 Converse Failure

The converse does not hold:

I(C; M_t) \ge R_C(\varepsilon) \;\;\not\Longrightarrow\;\; \exists\, q \in \mathcal Q_{\mathcal A} \text{ with } P_e(q) \le \varepsilon.

Sufficient information in memory does not guarantee that the biological architecture can extract it.

6.3 Execution-Level Failure

Even perfect class decoding does not guarantee regenerative success:

\boxed{
D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}(t) \le \varepsilon_C
\;\not\Longrightarrow\;
D_{\mathcal A}^{Y,*}(t) \le \varepsilon_Y.
}

A tissue could correctly identify “posterior” but fail to regenerate because downstream execution is defective.

6.4 Two Distinct Bottlenecks

The framework yields:

\boxed{
\text{Information bottleneck}
\quad+\quad
\text{architecture/execution bottleneck}.
}

These are formally distinct and experimentally separable.

