7. The Information Ladder

The framework organizes sufficiency into four levels:

\boxed{
\begin{array}{c}
\textbf{Level 1: Representation}\\
I(C; M_t)
\\[2mm]
\downarrow
\\
\textbf{Level 2: Class Decoding}\\
D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}
\\[2mm]
\downarrow
\\
\textbf{Level 3: Regenerative Execution}\\
D_{\mathcal A}^{Y,*}
\\[2mm]
\downarrow
\\
\textbf{Level 4: Regenerative Phenotype}\\
Y
\end{array}}

Class sufficiency and regenerative sufficiency are distinct:

\mathcal S_{\mathcal A}^{C}
=
\mathbf 1\left[ D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*} \le \varepsilon_C \right],
\qquad
\mathcal S_{\mathcal A}^{Y}
=
\mathbf 1\left[ \mathbb E[d_Y(Y, \hat Y)] \le \varepsilon_Y \right],

with

\boxed{
\mathcal S_{\mathcal A}^{C} = 1
\;\not\Rightarrow\;
\mathcal S_{\mathcal A}^{Y} = 1.
}

This prevents the identification of positional classification with regenerative success.

