8. Closed-Loop Active Memory

8.1 General Kernel Formulation

The decoder is not a passive reader; it actively maintains M_t via write-back. The correct formalism is a closed-loop active memory system with exogenous input:

\hat C_t \sim q_{\mathcal A}(\cdot \mid M_t),

M_{t+1} \sim P_M(\cdot \mid M_t, \hat C_t, U_t),

where P_M is a Markov transition kernel and U_t is an exogenous input carrying information about C from wound signals, neighboring cells, nerves, systemic factors, or global axes.

8.2 Exogenous-Information Requirement

If U_t is absent or uninformative about C, then C \to M_t \to \hat C_t \to M_{t+1} is Markov, and by the data-processing inequality,

\boxed{
I(C; M_{t+1}) \le I(C; \hat C_t) \le I(C; M_t).
}

Proposition. If the chain is Markov and U_t is uninformative, then task-relevant information is non-increasing:

I(C; M_{t+1}) \le I(C; M_t).

Therefore any increase I(C; M_{t+1}) > I(C; M_t) requires an additional information-bearing variable or a violation of the assumed Markov structure.

Remark. The condition I(C; U_t) > 0 is necessary for information generation but not sufficient:

I(C; U_t) = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; \text{no generation through } U_t,

but

I(C; U_t) > 0 \;\not\Rightarrow\; \Delta I > 0.

The decoder may fail to exploit U_t. Information availability does not imply architectural utilization. This asymmetry reinforces the central thesis.

8.3 Dynamical Decomposition

Define the net change in task-relevant information:

\Delta I(t) = I(C;M_t) - I(C;M_0).

The proposed decomposition is

\Delta I(t) = \Delta I_{\rm passive}(t) + \Delta I_{\rm active}(t) + \Delta I_{\rm interaction}(t),

where \Delta I_{\rm passive} corresponds to dilution, drift, and degradation; \Delta I_{\rm active} corresponds to active reconstruction driven by U_t; and \Delta I_{\rm interaction} captures non-additive coupling. This decomposition is a modeling proposal, not an identity; its validity is an empirical question.

8.4 Retention Time

Two distinct quantities are defined:

First-loss time:

\tau_{\downarrow}(\varepsilon) = \inf\{t > 0 : I(C;M_t) < R_C(\varepsilon)\}.

Persistent failure time:

\boxed{\,\tau_{\rm pers}(\varepsilon) = \inf\{t > 0 : \forall s \ge t,\ I(C;M_s) < R_C(\varepsilon)\}\,.}

For systems with active reconstruction, \tau_{\rm pers}(\varepsilon) \gg \tau_{\downarrow}(\varepsilon) is possible, and the gap between them quantifies the contribution of active memory maintenance.

