9. Causal Structure and Identifiability

9.1 Causal Graph

The framework requires an explicit causal DAG:

C
\rightarrow
M_t
\rightarrow
\hat C_t
\rightarrow
A_t
\rightarrow
Y,

with

U_t \rightarrow M_{t+1},
\qquad
\Theta_{\mathcal A} \rightarrow (\hat C_t, A_t, M_{t+1}, Y),
\qquad
\eta_t \rightarrow M_{t+1}.

Interventions:

do(M),
\qquad
do(\Theta_{\mathcal A}),
\qquad
do(U).

9.2 Measured Versus Biological Memory

The theory explicitly distinguishes

M_{\rm measured} \neq M_{\rm biological}.

Symmetry of the measured representation does not prove symmetry of the actual biological memory. A failure to detect asymmetry may reflect representational incompleteness rather than biological symmetry.

9.3 Perturbation Framework

Let \theta \in \Theta be a perturbation parameter. Define

I(C; M_t \mid \theta), \qquad D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}(t \mid \theta).

The causal effect on memory is

\Delta I(\theta) = I(C; M_t \mid \theta) - I(C; M_t \mid \theta_0),

and on decoder performance is

\Delta D(\theta) = D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}(t \mid \theta) - D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}(t \mid \theta_0).

9.4 Identifiability Criteria

Memory-side causality: |\Delta D(\theta)| < \delta_D and \Delta I(\theta) < -\delta_I.

Decoder-side causality: |\Delta I(\theta)| < \delta_I and \Delta D(\theta) > \delta_D.

Synergistic causality: \Delta I(\theta) < -\delta_I and \Delta D(\theta) > \delta_D.

Null result: |\Delta I(\theta)| < \delta_I and |\Delta D(\theta)| < \delta_D.

Here \delta_I, \delta_D > 0 are pre-registered equivalence margins.

9.5 Effect-Size Specification and Sensitivity

The equivalence margins are defined relative to the task rate-distortion threshold:

\delta_I = \eta_I \cdot R_C(\varepsilon), \qquad \delta_D = \eta_D \cdot \varepsilon,

with \eta_I = 0.1 and \eta_D = 0.1 pre-registered. The margins are pre-registered practical conventions, not theoretically derived constants. Sensitivity analysis is required over

\eta_I \in \{0.05, 0.10, 0.20\},
\qquad
\eta_D \in \{0.05, 0.10, 0.20\}.

If the biological conclusion changes qualitatively between 5% and 20% margins, this is scientifically informative.

9.6 Timescale Separation

Decoder perturbations may alter M_t at steady state even if I(C; M_t) is unchanged acutely.

Acute readout: measurement immediately after perturbation, before write-back has altered M_t.

Steady-state readout: measurement after closed-loop equilibration.

The identifiability criteria apply separately at each timescale. All experiments must pre-register which readout is used.

