12. Empirical Program

12.1 Phase 1 — Formalize the Task-Specific Representation

Specify the partition \phi, the prior \mathbf p, and the diagnostic \eta_{\rm task}. Derive optimal class boundaries for given regenerative responses. Pre-register the memory representation family \{M_t^{(j)}\}.

12.2 Phase 2 — Parameterize the Architecture Class

Specify the coordinates of \theta = (\theta_{\rm ch}, \theta_{\rm fb}, \theta_{\rm cell}) and the admissible set \Theta_{\mathcal A}. For the axolotl, \theta_{\rm fb} includes Hand2–Shh loop gain and gap-junction conductance; \theta_{\rm cell} includes posterior-competent cell density. For killifish, \theta_{\rm ch} includes TRACS-mediated ECM signaling parameters. For planarians, \theta_{\rm cell} includes neoblast recruitment rate and muscle–neoblast coupling. Establish functional identifiability.

12.3 Phase 3 — Compute Rate-Distortion Thresholds

For K classes and target error \varepsilon, compute R_C(\varepsilon) under Hamming distortion for candidate priors. Compute R_{C \to Y}(\varepsilon) for the regenerative outcome. Tabulate \tau_{\rm pers}(\varepsilon) for candidate priors and estimate the gap between \tau_{\downarrow}(\varepsilon) and \tau_{\rm pers}(\varepsilon).

12.4 Phase 4 — Estimate I(C; M_t)

Using available transcriptomic and chromatin data, estimate mutual information between candidate memory observables and regenerative classes via the hierarchical Bayesian model of §11.2. Report lower bounds and posterior credible intervals.

12.5 Phase 5 — Memory Representation Family Analysis

For each candidate representation M_t^{(j)}, compute I(C; M_t^{(j)}). Compute conditional informations

\Delta I_{j|k} = I(C; M_t^{(j)} \mid M_t^{(k)})

to test whether representation j provides task-relevant information beyond representation k.

12.6 Phase 6 — Design the sqstm1 Test

Plan statistical comparison of I(C; M_t) and D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*} before and after sqstm1 deletion. Use equivalence testing with margin \delta_I = \eta_I \cdot R_C(\varepsilon). Specify \delta_D = \eta_D \cdot \varepsilon. Perform simulation-based assurance.

12.7 Phase 7 — Cross-Species Comparison

Apply identical formal quantities to axolotl, killifish, and planarian data. Test whether sufficiency resides memory-side or decoder-side across systems.

12.8 Phase 8 — State-Dependent Architecture in Axolotl

Otsuki et al. report that Shh can posteriorize positional memory in blastema cells but not mature uninjured cells, and that the Hand2–Shh loop can persist after Shh removal. This motivates the hypothesis

\Theta_{\mathcal A}^{\rm mature} \neq \Theta_{\mathcal A}^{\rm blastema}.

Test whether memory plasticity itself is state-dependent by comparing I(C; M_{\rm mature}) and I(C; M_{\rm blastema}) under forced Shh.

