13. Refutable Predictions

13.1 Prediction 1 — sqstm1 Decoupling

sqstm1 deletion increases regenerative capacity of distal injuries. Under the framework this could arise from three distinct channels: a change in I(C; M_t), a change in \Theta_{\mathcal A}, or a change in noise.

Hypothesis generated by the framework. sqstm1 deletion should leave I(C; M_t) statistically unchanged while reducing D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}(t).

Refutable prediction. Using equivalence testing with pre-registered margin \delta_I = 0.1 \cdot R_C(\varepsilon):

|\Delta I(C; M)| < \delta_I
\quad \land \quad
\Delta D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*} < -\delta_D.

Refutation. If I(C; M_t) significantly decreases and D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}(t) decreases in parallel, this would be consistent with a memory-side contribution and would reject the specific null hypothesis of architecture-only modulation. If neither quantity changes, the proposed channel decomposition is incomplete.

13.2 Prediction 2 — Anteroposterior Asymmetry

Anterior-to-posterior reprogramming is more facile than reverse. Under the framework this asymmetry must reside either in I(C; M_t) or in D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}.

Refutable prediction. Measurement of I(C; M_t) and D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*} before and after anterior-to-posterior versus posterior-to-anterior reprogramming yields:

I(C; M) D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*} Interpretation
Asymmetric Symmetric Memory contribution
Symmetric Asymmetric Decoder contribution
Asymmetric Asymmetric Coupled contribution
Symmetric Symmetric Representational failure or unmeasured variable

Refutation. If both measured quantities are symmetric and reprogrammability is nonetheless asymmetric, the framework's decomposition is refuted—unless the fifth possibility of representational incompleteness is confirmed.

13.3 Prediction 3 — Planarian Stem Cell Dependence

Positional information in muscle guides neoblast-mediated regeneration. Without neoblasts, regenerative output fails. Under the framework, this is a candidate test of whether regenerative failure can occur despite persistence of positional information in muscle.

Refutable prediction. In neoblast-depleted planarians, I(C; M_{\rm muscle}) should remain significantly above R_C(\varepsilon), while regenerative output collapses.

Refutation. If I(C; M_{\rm muscle}) collapses alongside regenerative failure, this provides evidence against the hypothesis that muscle-resident positional information remains sufficient after neoblast depletion. It does not uniquely establish memory dependence on neoblasts, since irradiation may indirectly alter muscle state, the chosen representation may be incomplete, or the muscle signal may depend dynamically on neoblast feedback.

Chance-level correction. The correct chance-level error is

P_e^{\rm chance} = 1 - \max_i p(c_i).

The critical test requires a timecourse showing I(C; M_{\rm muscle}) above threshold while P_e approaches chance level.

