Appendix B: Proofs of Core Theorems

B.1 Information Impossibility Theorem

Theorem B.1. Let C have distribution p(c) on \mathcal C = \{c_1, \dots, c_K\}. Let

R_C(\varepsilon) = \inf_{P_{\hat C \mid C}:\, P_e \le \varepsilon} I(C; \hat C).

Let M_t be any memory state jointly distributed with C, and let q_{\mathcal A}(\hat c \mid m) be any decoder in the admissible architecture class \mathcal Q_{\mathcal A}. Then

I(C; M_t) < R_C(\varepsilon) \quad \Longrightarrow \quad P_e(q_{\mathcal A}) > \varepsilon.

Proof. Let q_{\mathcal A} be any decoder. Define the Markov chain

C \to M_t \to \hat C.

By the data-processing inequality,

I(C; \hat C) \le I(C; M_t).

Suppose, for contradiction, that P_e(q_{\mathcal A}) \le \varepsilon. Then q_{\mathcal A} is a feasible test channel for the rate-distortion minimization, so by definition of R_C(\varepsilon),

R_C(\varepsilon) \le I(C; \hat C).

Combining the two inequalities,

R_C(\varepsilon) \le I(C; \hat C) \le I(C; M_t),

which contradicts I(C; M_t) < R_C(\varepsilon). Therefore P_e(q_{\mathcal A}) > \varepsilon. \square

B.2 Converse Failure

Proposition B.2. The converse implication

I(C; M_t) \ge R_C(\varepsilon) \quad \Longrightarrow \quad \exists\, q \in \mathcal Q_{\mathcal A} : P_e(q) \le \varepsilon

does not hold.

Counterexample. Let \mathcal C = \{0, 1\} with uniform prior, so H(C) = 1 bit. Let \varepsilon = 0. Then R_C(0) = H(C) = 1 bit, since perfect reconstruction requires full information.

Let M_t be binary with I(C; M_t) = 1 bit. This requires M_t to determine C bijectively. Suppose, however, that the admissible architecture \mathcal Q_{\mathcal A} contains only the constant decoder

q_{\mathcal A}(\hat c \mid m) = \frac{1}{2} \quad \forall\, m.

Then P_e(q_{\mathcal A}) = 1/2 > 0 = \varepsilon. The memory contains sufficient information, but the architecture cannot extract it. \square

B.3 Execution-Level Failure

Proposition B.3. Even perfect class decoding does not guarantee regenerative success:

D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}(t) \le \varepsilon_C \;\not\Longrightarrow\; D_{\mathcal A}^{Y,*}(t) \le \varepsilon_Y.

Proof. Let \hat C_t = C almost surely. The regenerative outcome is generated by

A_t \sim P_{A \mid C}, \qquad Y_{t+\Delta} \sim P_{Y \mid A, C}.

Even with \hat C_t = C, the execution kernel P_{A \mid C} may be defective, producing Y with \mathbb E[d_Y(Y, \hat Y)] > \varepsilon_Y. The class decoding error is zero, but the regenerative error is positive. \square

B.4 Monotonicity of R_C(\varepsilon)

Proposition B.4. The rate-distortion function R_C(\varepsilon) is non-increasing in \varepsilon, convex, and satisfies

R_C(0) = H(C), \qquad R_C(\varepsilon) = 0 \text{ for } \varepsilon \ge 1 - \max_i p(c_i).

Proof. Non-increasing: if \varepsilon_1 \le \varepsilon_2, the feasible set for \varepsilon_1 is a subset of the feasible set for \varepsilon_2, so the infimum over the larger set is no greater.

Convexity: standard result for rate-distortion functions (Berger, 1971).

Boundary conditions: at \varepsilon = 0, perfect reconstruction requires I(C; \hat C) = H(C). At \varepsilon \ge 1 - \max_i p(c_i), the constant channel P_{\hat C \mid C}(\hat c \mid c) = p(\hat c) achieves expected distortion 1 - \max_i p(c_i) with I(C; \hat C) = 0. \square

B.5 Exogenous-Information Requirement

Proposition B.5. If C \to M_t \to \hat C_t \to M_{t+1} is Markov and U_t is uninformative about C given M_t, then

I(C; M_{t+1}) \le I(C; M_t).

Proof. Since U_t is conditionally independent of C given M_t, the chain

C \to M_t \to (\hat C_t, U_t) \to M_{t+1}

is Markov. By the data-processing inequality,

I(C; M_{t+1}) \le I(C; \hat C_t, U_t) = I(C; \hat C_t) + I(C; U_t \mid \hat C_t).

Since U_t is conditionally independent of C given M_t, and \hat C_t is a function of M_t,

I(C; U_t \mid \hat C_t) \le I(C; U_t \mid M_t) = 0.

Therefore,

I(C; M_{t+1}) \le I(C; \hat C_t) \le I(C; M_t).

\square

Remark B.6. The condition I(C; U_t) > 0 is necessary for information generation but not sufficient:

I(C; U_t) = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; \text{no generation through } U_t,

but

I(C; U_t) > 0 \;\not\Rightarrow\; \Delta I > 0.

The decoder may fail to exploit U_t. Information availability does not imply architectural utilization.

