Appendix C: Generalized Fano Bound with Non-Uniform Priors

C.1 Standard Fano Inequality

Theorem C.1. Let C and \hat C be random variables on a set of size K. Define the error event E = \mathbf 1[C \neq \hat C] with P_e = \mathbb P(E = 1). Then

H(C \mid \hat C) \le h(P_e) + P_e \log_2(K - 1).

Proof. Let E be the error indicator. By the chain rule,

H(C, E \mid \hat C) = H(E \mid \hat C) + H(C \mid E, \hat C).

On one hand, H(E \mid \hat C) \le h(P_e). On the other hand,

H(C \mid E, \hat C) = (1 - P_e) H(C \mid E = 0, \hat C) + P_e H(C \mid E = 1, \hat C).

When E = 0, C = \hat C deterministically, so H(C \mid E = 0, \hat C) = 0. When E = 1, C takes values in \mathcal C \setminus \{\hat C\}, a set of size at most K - 1, so H(C \mid E = 1, \hat C) \le \log_2(K - 1). Therefore,

H(C \mid E, \hat C) \le P_e \log_2(K - 1).

Combining,

H(C \mid \hat C) \le H(E \mid \hat C) + H(C \mid E, \hat C) \le h(P_e) + P_e \log_2(K - 1).

\square

C.2 Bound on I(C; M_t)

Corollary C.2. Since C \to M_t \to \hat C is Markov,

H(C \mid M_t) \le H(C \mid \hat C) \le h(P_e) + P_e \log_2(K-1).

Therefore,

I(C; M_t) = H(C) - H(C \mid M_t) \ge H(C) - h(P_e) - P_e \log_2(K-1).

C.3 Asymptotic Behavior under Information Loss

Suppose I(C; M_t) \to 0 as t \to \infty. Then C and M_t become asymptotically independent, so the optimal decoder is the maximum-a-posteriori (MAP) decoder

\hat c^*(m) = \arg\max_{c} p(c),

which is constant in m. The resulting error is

P_e^{\rm chance} = 1 - \max_i p(c_i).

For uniform priors, p(c_i) = 1/K, so P_e^{\rm chance} = 1 - 1/K.

C.4 Tightness

The Fano bound is tight in the limit P_e \to 0, where

H(C \mid M_t) \approx P_e \log_2(K-1) + h(P_e),

and in the limit P_e \to P_e^{\rm chance}, where

H(C \mid M_t) \approx H(C).

In intermediate regimes, the bound may be loose. The rate-distortion formulation provides a tighter characterization.

