Appendix D: Rate-Distortion Theory for Non-Uniform Priors

D.1 Rate-Distortion Function

For a source C \sim p(c) on \mathcal C = \{c_1, \dots, c_K\} and Hamming distortion d_C(c, \hat c) = \mathbf 1[c \neq \hat c],

R_C(D) = \min_{P_{\hat C \mid C}:\, \mathbb E[d_C(C, \hat C)] \le D} I(C; \hat C).

D.2 KKT Derivation of the Optimal Test Channel

Introduce the Lagrangian with multiplier \lambda \ge 0 for the distortion constraint and multipliers \mu(c) for the row-sum constraints:

\mathcal L = I(C; \hat C) + \lambda \left( \sum_{c,\hat c} p(c) P_{\hat C \mid C}(\hat c \mid c) d_C(c, \hat c) - D \right) - \sum_c \mu(c) \left( \sum_{\hat c} P_{\hat C \mid C}(\hat c \mid c) - 1 \right).

The stationarity condition \partial \mathcal L / \partial P_{\hat C \mid C}(\hat c \mid c) = 0 gives

p(c)\left[ \log_2 \frac{P_{\hat C \mid C}(\hat c \mid c)}{p(\hat c)} + \frac{1}{\ln 2} \right] + \lambda\, p(c)\, d_C(c, \hat c) - \mu(c) = 0.

Solving for P_{\hat C \mid C}(\hat c \mid c) and normalizing over \hat c yields the fixed-point relation

\boxed{\;P_{\hat C \mid C}(\hat c \mid c) = \frac{p(\hat c)\, e^{-\lambda\, d_C(c, \hat c)}}{\displaystyle\sum_{\hat c'} p(\hat c')\, e^{-\lambda\, d_C(c, \hat c')}}\;}

with \lambda chosen so that the distortion constraint is met with equality. For Hamming distortion, this specializes to

P_{\hat C \mid C}(c \mid c) = \frac{p(c)}{p(c) + (1 - p(c))\, e^{-\lambda}},

P_{\hat C \mid C}(\hat c \mid c) = \frac{p(\hat c)\, e^{-\lambda}}{p(c) + (1 - p(c))\, e^{-\lambda}}, \qquad \hat c \neq c.

This channel is not symmetric within the error event when p(c) is non-uniform.

D.3 Uniform Prior: Closed Form

For uniform p(c) = 1/K,

R_C(D) = \log_2 K - h(D) - D \log_2(K-1), \qquad 0 \le D \le 1 - \frac{1}{K}.

This is achieved by a symmetric test channel with

P_{\hat C \mid C}(\hat c \mid c) = \begin{cases} 1 - D, & \hat c = c, \\ \dfrac{D}{K-1}, & \hat c \neq c. \end{cases}

D.4 Binary Non-Uniform: Closed Form

For K = 2 with p(c_1) = p, p(c_2) = 1-p,

\boxed{
R_C(D) =
\begin{cases}
h(p) - h(D), & 0 \le D < \min(p, 1-p), \\
0, & D \ge \min(p, 1-p).
\end{cases}
}

The optimal test channel is

P_{\hat C \mid C}(\hat c \mid c) = \begin{cases} 1 - D, & \hat c = c, \\ D, & \hat c \neq c. \end{cases}

In this case, symmetry within the error event does hold, but only because K = 2. For K \ge 3 and non-uniform p(c), symmetry fails in general.

D.5 Non-Uniform Prior: Parametric Solution

For non-uniform p(c), the optimal test channel satisfies the KKT conditions. Let

\phi(c) = \sum_{\hat c} P_{\hat C \mid C}(\hat c \mid c) e^{-\lambda \mathbf 1[\hat c \neq c]}.

The optimal channel is

P_{\hat C \mid C}(\hat c \mid c) = \frac{p(\hat c) e^{-\lambda \mathbf 1[\hat c \neq c]}}{\phi(c)},

where p(\hat c) = \sum_c p(c) P_{\hat C \mid C}(\hat c \mid c) is the marginal. The parameter \lambda is chosen so that

\mathbb E[d_C(C, \hat C)] = \sum_c p(c) \left(1 - P_{\hat C \mid C}(c \mid c)\right) = D.

D.6 Threshold Definition

For target error \varepsilon, define

R_C(\varepsilon) = R_C(D) \big|_{D = \varepsilon}.

---

Appendix E: Blahut–Arimoto Algorithm and Validation

E.1 Algorithm Specification

Input: prior vector p \in \Delta^{K-1}, target distortion D \in (0, D_{\max}], tolerance \epsilon_{\rm BA} > 0, outer tolerance \epsilon_\lambda > 0.

Output: R_C(D) and the optimal channel P^\star_{\hat C \mid C}.

Procedure.

1. Bracket \lambda. Set \lambda_{\rm lo} = 0, \lambda_{\rm hi} = \Lambda_{\max}, where \Lambda_{\max} is chosen so that D(\Lambda_{\max}) < D. A safe default is \Lambda_{\max} = 10^3 with log-domain exponentiation.
2. Outer bisection on \lambda. Repeat until |\lambda_{\rm hi} - \lambda_{\rm lo}| < \epsilon_\lambda:
   1. Set \lambda = (\lambda_{\rm lo} + \lambda_{\rm hi})/2.
   2. Initialize p^{(0)}(\hat c) = 1/K.
   3. Inner Blahut–Arimoto iteration. Repeat until \|p^{(n+1)} - p^{(n)}\|_1 < \epsilon_{\rm BA}:
      P^{(n+1)}(\hat c \mid c) = \frac{p^{(n)}(\hat c)\, e^{-\lambda d_C(c,\hat c)}}{\sum_{\hat c'} p^{(n)}(\hat c')\, e^{-\lambda d_C(c,\hat c')}},
      p^{(n+1)}(\hat c) = \sum_c p(c)\, P^{(n+1)}(\hat c \mid c).
   4. Compute D(\lambda) = \sum_{c,\hat c} p(c) P(\hat c \mid c) d_C(c,\hat c).
   5. If D(\lambda) > D, set \lambda_{\rm lo} = \lambda; else set \lambda_{\rm hi} = \lambda. The direction of the bisection update follows from the monotonicity: D(\lambda) is decreasing in \lambda.
3. Return. Set P^\star = P^{(n+1)} from the final inner loop and R_C(D) = I(C; \hat C) evaluated on P^\star.

Trivial-bound handling. If D \ge D_{\max}, return R_C(D) = 0 and the constant channel

P^\star_{\hat C \mid C}(\hat c \mid c) = p(\hat c) \quad \forall\, c.

E.2 Numerical Stability

Log-domain exponentiation. For \lambda \gtrsim 700, \exp(-\lambda) underflows in float64. Since Hamming distortion yields e^{-\lambda \cdot 0} = 1 on the diagonal, the update remains stable as long as \lambda < 700. For the range K \le 10 and \varepsilon \ge 10^{-2}, the required \lambda satisfies \lambda \le 200. We fix \Lambda_{\max} = 10^3 and compute exponentials in the log domain when \lambda > 500:

\log P(\hat c \mid c) = \log p(\hat c) - \lambda d_C(c, \hat c) - \log \sum_{\hat c'} \exp\!\left( \log p(\hat c') - \lambda d_C(c, \hat c') \right),

followed by exponentiation after subtracting the row-wise maximum (log-sum-exp trick).

Mutual-information evaluation. Direct evaluation of I(C; \hat C) with a numerical regularizer biases the estimate. We instead sum only over the support:

I(C; \hat C) = \sum_{(c, \hat c)\,:\, p(c) P(\hat c \mid c) > 0} p(c) P(\hat c \mid c) \log_2 \frac{P(\hat c \mid c)}{p(\hat c)}.

Convergence. The inner Blahut–Arimoto iteration converges geometrically with rate governed by \lambda. For Hamming distortion and K \le 10, 10^3 inner iterations suffice for \epsilon_{\rm BA} = 10^{-8}. The outer bisection converges linearly with 50 iterations reaching \epsilon_\lambda \approx 10^{-15} on \lambda \in [0, 10^3].

E.3 Validation Suite

A numerical implementation is credible only if it reproduces the following cases to within 10^{-6} in absolute rate.

E.3.1 Binary uniform prior. For p = (1/2, 1/2),

R_C(D) = 1 - h(D), \qquad 0 \le D \le 1/2.

E.3.2 General uniform prior. For p(c_i) = 1/K,

R_C(D) = \log_2 K - h(D) - D \log_2(K-1), \qquad 0 \le D \le 1 - 1/K.

Test at K \in \{3, 5, 10\}.

E.3.3 Binary non-uniform prior. For p = (p, 1-p),

R_C(D) = h(p) - h(D), \qquad 0 \le D < \min(p, 1-p),

with R_C(D) = 0 for D \ge \min(p, 1-p). Test at p \in \{0.6, 0.75, 0.9\}.

E.3.4 Boundary conditions.

R_C(0) = H(C) = -\sum_i p(c_i) \log_2 p(c_i),

R_C(D_{\max}) = 0 \quad \text{with} \quad D_{\max} = 1 - \max_i p(c_i).

Monotonicity: R_C(D_1) \ge R_C(D_2) for D_1 \le D_2. Convexity: R_C(\alpha D_1 + (1-\alpha) D_2) \le \alpha R_C(D_1) + (1-\alpha) R_C(D_2).

E.3.5 Numerical convergence. For all test cases, the inner iteration must satisfy \|p^{(n+1)} - p^{(n)}\|_1 < 10^{-8} within 5000 iterations, and the outer bisection must achieve |D(\lambda) - D_{\rm target}| < 10^{-7} within 60 iterations.

