Appendix F: Task-Conditioned Regenerative Rate-Distortion

F.1 Inadequacy of Naive R_Y(\varepsilon)

The formulation

R_Y(\varepsilon) = \inf_{P_{\hat Y \mid Y}:\, \mathbb E[d_Y(Y,\hat Y)] \le \varepsilon} I(Y; \hat Y)

is not appropriate for the biological system. There is no source Y that is encoded and reconstructed. The correct formulation is task-conditioned: how much information about the positional class C is necessary to achieve an acceptable regenerative outcome?

F.2 Class-Conditioned Regenerative Rate-Distortion

Define the class-conditioned regenerative rate-distortion function:

\boxed{
R_{C \to Y}(\varepsilon)
=
\inf_{P_{\hat C \mid C}:\,
\mathbb E[d_Y(Y, \hat Y(\hat C))] \le \varepsilon}
I(C; \hat C).
}

Here \hat Y(\hat C) is the regenerative outcome induced by the decoded class \hat C, and d_Y is a distortion measure on outcomes—normalized limb-length discrepancy, skeletal-pattern mismatch, or functional score.

F.3 Biological Task Loss Formulation

Define the biological task loss L(C, A, Y) and the regenerative rate-distortion function

\boxed{
R_{\rm regen}(\varepsilon)
=
\inf_{P_{A \mid C}:\,
\mathbb E[L(C, A, Y)] \le \varepsilon}
I(C; A).
}

Then

I(C; M_t) < R_{\rm regen}(\varepsilon)
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\text{no decoder operating on } M_t \text{ can achieve the target regenerative performance.}

F.4 Relation to Class Rate-Distortion

The class rate-distortion threshold R_C(\varepsilon) is an intermediate necessary condition; R_{C \to Y}(\varepsilon) is the ultimate constraint on the full chain. In general,

R_{C \to Y}(\varepsilon) \ge R_C(\varepsilon)

because the regenerative outcome imposes additional constraints beyond class decoding.

F.5 Execution-Level Impossibility

Theorem F.1. Let D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}(t) be the optimal class decoding error and D_{\mathcal A}^{Y,*}(t) the optimal regenerative error. Then

D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}(t) \le \varepsilon_C \;\not\Longrightarrow\; D_{\mathcal A}^{Y,*}(t) \le \varepsilon_Y.

Proof. Let \hat C_t = C almost surely. The regenerative outcome is generated by

A_t \sim P_{A \mid C}, \qquad Y_{t+\Delta} \sim P_{Y \mid A, C}.

Even with \hat C_t = C, the execution kernel P_{A \mid C} may be defective, producing Y with \mathbb E[d_Y(Y, \hat Y)] > \varepsilon_Y. The class decoding error is zero, but the regenerative error is positive. \square

