Appendix G: Closed-Loop Active Memory

G.1 General Kernel Formulation

\hat C_t \sim q_{\mathcal A}(\cdot \mid M_t),

M_{t+1} \sim P_M(\cdot \mid M_t, \hat C_t, U_t).

G.2 Exogenous-Information Requirement

Proposition G.1. If U_t is absent or uninformative about C given M_t, then

I(C; M_{t+1}) \le I(C; M_t).

Proof. Since U_t is conditionally independent of C given M_t, the chain

C \to M_t \to (\hat C_t, U_t) \to M_{t+1}

is Markov. By the data-processing inequality,

I(C; M_{t+1}) \le I(C; \hat C_t, U_t) = I(C; \hat C_t) + I(C; U_t \mid \hat C_t).

Since U_t is conditionally independent of C given M_t, and \hat C_t is a function of M_t,

I(C; U_t \mid \hat C_t) \le I(C; U_t \mid M_t) = 0.

Therefore,

I(C; M_{t+1}) \le I(C; \hat C_t) \le I(C; M_t).

\square

Remark G.2. The condition I(C; U_t) > 0 is necessary for information generation but not sufficient:

I(C; U_t) = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; \text{no generation through } U_t,

but

I(C; U_t) > 0 \;\not\Rightarrow\; \Delta I > 0.

The decoder may fail to exploit U_t. Information availability does not imply architectural utilization.

G.3 Dynamical Decomposition

\Delta I(t) = I(C;M_t) - I(C;M_0) = \Delta I_{\rm passive}(t) + \Delta I_{\rm active}(t) + \Delta I_{\rm interaction}(t).

This decomposition is a modeling proposal, not an identity; its validity is an empirical question.

G.4 Retention Time

First-loss time:

\tau_{\downarrow}(\varepsilon) = \inf\{t > 0 : I(C;M_t) < R_C(\varepsilon)\}.

Persistent failure time:

\tau_{\rm pers}(\varepsilon) = \inf\{t > 0 : \forall s \ge t,\ I(C;M_s) < R_C(\varepsilon)\}.

Proposition G.3. If I(C; M_t) is non-increasing, then \tau_{\downarrow}(\varepsilon) = \tau_{\rm pers}(\varepsilon).

Proposition G.4. If I(C; M_t) is not monotone, then \tau_{\downarrow}(\varepsilon) \le \tau_{\rm pers}(\varepsilon), with strict inequality possible when active reconstruction restores I(C; M_t) above threshold after a transient dip.

G.5 Active Maintenance Extends Retention

Suppose the passive dynamics satisfy

I_{\rm passive}(t) = I(C; M_0) e^{-\gamma t},

and active reconstruction adds

I_{\rm active}(t) = \alpha \left(1 - e^{-\beta t}\right) I(C; M_0),

with \alpha, \beta, \gamma > 0. Then the net information is

I(C; M_t) = I(C; M_0) \left[ e^{-\gamma t} + \alpha \left(1 - e^{-\beta t}\right) \right].

The threshold crossing time \tau_{\rm pers}(\varepsilon) satisfies

e^{-\gamma \tau} + \alpha \left(1 - e^{-\beta \tau}\right) = \frac{R_C(\varepsilon)}{I(C; M_0)}.

For \alpha > 0 and \beta > \gamma, active maintenance substantially extends \tau_{\rm pers}(\varepsilon) relative to the passive case \alpha = 0.

