Appendix H: Identifiability and Causal Decomposition

H.1 Causal Graph

The framework requires an explicit causal DAG:

C \rightarrow M_t \rightarrow \hat C_t \rightarrow A_t \rightarrow Y,

with

U_t \rightarrow M_{t+1},
\qquad
\Theta_{\mathcal A} \rightarrow (\hat C_t, A_t, M_{t+1}, Y),
\qquad
\eta_t \rightarrow M_{t+1}.

Interventions:

do(M), \qquad do(\Theta_{\mathcal A}), \qquad do(U).

H.2 Measured Versus Biological Memory

The theory explicitly distinguishes

M_{\rm measured} \neq M_{\rm biological}.

Symmetry of the measured representation does not prove symmetry of the actual biological memory. A failure to detect asymmetry may reflect representational incompleteness rather than biological symmetry.

H.3 Three Sources of Regenerative Failure

1. Memory-side failure: I(C; M_t) falls below R_C(\varepsilon).
2. Decoder-side failure: I(C; M_t) \ge R_C(\varepsilon) but D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}(t) > \varepsilon.
3. Noise-side failure: \eta_t is large enough to make D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}(t) > \varepsilon even with adequate memory.

H.4 Perturbation Framework

Let \theta \in \Theta be a perturbation parameter. Define

I(C; M_t \mid \theta), \qquad D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}(t \mid \theta).

The causal effect on memory is

\Delta I(\theta) = I(C; M_t \mid \theta) - I(C; M_t \mid \theta_0),

and on decoder performance is

\Delta D(\theta) = D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}(t \mid \theta) - D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}(t \mid \theta_0).

H.5 Identifiability Criteria

Memory-side causality: |\Delta D(\theta)| < \delta_D and \Delta I(\theta) < -\delta_I.

Decoder-side causality: |\Delta I(\theta)| < \delta_I and \Delta D(\theta) > \delta_D.

Synergistic causality: \Delta I(\theta) < -\delta_I and \Delta D(\theta) > \delta_D.

Null result: |\Delta I(\theta)| < \delta_I and |\Delta D(\theta)| < \delta_D.

Here \delta_I, \delta_D > 0 are pre-registered equivalence margins.

H.6 Effect-Size Specification and Sensitivity

\delta_I = \eta_I \cdot R_C(\varepsilon), \qquad \delta_D = \eta_D \cdot \varepsilon,

with \eta_I = 0.1 and \eta_D = 0.1 pre-registered. The margins are pre-registered practical conventions, not theoretically derived constants. Sensitivity analysis is required over

\eta_I \in \{0.05, 0.10, 0.20\}, \qquad \eta_D \in \{0.05, 0.10, 0.20\}.

If the biological conclusion changes qualitatively between 5% and 20% margins, this is scientifically informative.

H.7 Equivalence Testing

To claim |\Delta I(\theta)| < \delta_I, we use two one-sided tests (TOST):

H_0^-: \Delta I(\theta) \le -\delta_I, \qquad H_0^+: \Delta I(\theta) \ge \delta_I.

Rejecting both null hypotheses at level \alpha establishes equivalence within margin \delta_I.

H.8 Timescale Separation

Decoder perturbations may alter M_t at steady state even if I(C; M_t) is unchanged acutely.

Acute readout: measurement immediately after perturbation, before write-back has altered M_t.

Steady-state readout: measurement after closed-loop equilibration.

The identifiability criteria apply separately at each timescale. All experiments must pre-register which readout is used.

H.9 Interaction Effects

For joint perturbations \theta_1, \theta_2, define the interaction

\Delta^2 I(\theta_1, \theta_2) = \Delta I(\theta_1, \theta_2) - \Delta I(\theta_1) - \Delta I(\theta_2).

If \Delta^2 I(\theta_1, \theta_2) \neq 0, the perturbations interact non-additively. This is expected when memory and decoder are coupled through the closed-loop write-back.

H.10 Functional Identifiability

Parameterizing \Theta_{\mathcal A} does not automatically make D_{\mathcal A}^*(t) estimable. Multiple parameter combinations can generate the same observed data:

P_\theta(Y \mid M) = P_{\theta'}(Y \mid M) \quad\Rightarrow\quad \theta \sim \theta'.

Define functional identifiability:

\theta \sim_{\rm func} \theta' \iff D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}(\theta) = D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}(\theta').

The framework requires only identification of the equivalence class determining decoding performance, not estimation of every biological parameter individually.

