Appendix I: Estimation Theory for Mutual Information

I.1 Problem Statement

Given n i.i.d. samples \{(C_i, M_i)\}_{i=1}^n from P_{CM}, estimate I(C; M). In biological applications, M is high-dimensional and n is limited.

I.2 Estimator Classification

The estimators are not interchangeable:

· Primary: Cross-validated classifier-based lower bound via Fano inversion.
· Secondary: kNN/KSG-style estimator where dimensionality permits.
· Representation-learning: InfoNCE, only with careful negative-sampling design.
· Bound validation: Fano-based lower bound.

All estimators provide lower bounds or estimates:

\boxed{I_{\rm lower} \le I(C; M).}

No single estimator should be presented as “the mutual information.”

I.3 Variational Lower Bounds

InfoNCE (contrastive estimation). Let f_\theta: \mathcal M \to \mathbb R^K be a neural network. The InfoNCE bound is

I(C; M) \ge \mathbb E\left[ \log \frac{e^{f_\theta(M; C)}}{\frac{1}{n} \sum_{j=1}^n e^{f_\theta(M_j; C)}} \right] + \log n,

where the expectation is over positive pairs (C, M) and n-1 negative samples M_j.

Donsker–Varadhan. For any function g: \mathcal C \times \mathcal M \to \mathbb R,

I(C; M) \ge \sup_g \mathbb E[g(C, M)] - \log \mathbb E[e^{g(C, M')}],

where M' is an independent copy of M.

I.4 Classifier-Based Fano Inversion

Train a classifier q_\theta(\hat c \mid m) to predict C from M. Let \hat P_e be the empirical error on held-out data. Then

I(C; M) \ge H(C) - h(\hat P_e) - \hat P_e \log_2(K-1).

I.5 k-Nearest-Neighbor Estimation

The Kozachenko–Leonenko estimator for continuous M:

\hat I(C; M) = \psi(n) - \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \mathbb E\left[ \psi(n_{c_i}) \right],

where \psi is the digamma function and n_{c_i} is the number of samples within the k-NN ball of M_i that share class c_i.

I.6 Estimation Protocol

· Train/test separation: D_{\rm train} \cap D_{\rm test} = \varnothing.
· Cross-validation with permutation nulls, randomizing C while preserving batch structure.
· Bootstrap confidence intervals across biological replicates, not individual cells.
· Batch correction across amputation positions and time points.
· Explicit definition of M_t = f(Z_t) to prevent latent-variable inflation.
· Validation on simulated data with known I(C; M).

I.7 Bias–Variance Tradeoff

For finite n, variational estimators of I(C; M) are biased. The bias scales as

\text{Bias}[\hat I] = O\left(\frac{d}{n}\right),

where d is the effective dimension of M. To control bias: reduce d via dimensionality reduction; increase n via biological replicates; use hold-out validation to detect overfitting.

