Appendix J: Hierarchical Bayesian Estimation

J.1 Inadequacy of Gaussian Model for MI

Mutual information is not naturally Gaussian. The correct approach is to model the underlying conditional distribution and derive I(C; M) from the posterior.

J.2 Generative Model for the Decoder

\operatorname{logit} P(C = c \mid M, Z) = \alpha_c + \beta_c^\top M + u_{\rm animal, c} + u_{\rm batch, c},

where u_{\rm animal, c} and u_{\rm batch, c} are random effects with hierarchical priors:

u_{\rm animal, c} \sim \mathcal N(0, \sigma_{\rm animal}^2), \qquad u_{\rm batch, c} \sim \mathcal N(0, \sigma_{\rm batch}^2).

J.3 Derivation of I(C; M) from Posterior

Posterior samples of the conditional distribution give

I(C; M) = \sum_{c, m} p(c, m) \log \frac{p(c, m)}{p(c) p(m)}.

Alternatively, estimate I(C; M) from the posterior predictive distribution using a variational representation.

J.4 Partial Pooling

The hierarchical model is expected to improve precision through partial pooling; its impact on required biological replication will be evaluated through simulation-based assurance, not assumed.

---

Appendix K: Simulation-Based Assurance

K.1 Inadequacy of Analytic Sample-Size Formula

The analytic formula

n \gtrsim \frac{(z_{1-\alpha} + z_{1-\beta})^2 \sigma_I^2}{\delta_I^2}

is not valid for equivalence testing with hierarchical Bayesian estimation.

K.2 Simulation-Based Assurance Procedure

1. Simulate biological replicates.
2. Generate molecular measurements.
3. Impose known \Delta I.
4. Estimate MI using the complete pipeline.
5. Fit the hierarchical model.
6. Perform the equivalence criterion.
7. Repeat 1,000–10,000 times.
8. Determine n yielding 80% or 90% probability of correctly declaring equivalence.

The result is reported as

P(\text{correct decision} \mid n) = 0.8.

K.3 Power Analysis for the sqstm1 Test

For target power 1-\beta = 0.8, significance \alpha = 0.05, and effect size \Delta I = 0.2 bits, the required number of biological replicates under the hierarchical model is determined by simulation. Partial pooling is expected to improve precision, but its impact on required biological replication must be evaluated through simulation, not assumed.

