L.1 The Two Bottlenecks

\boxed{\text{Information bottleneck} \quad+\quad \text{architecture/execution bottleneck}.}

Formally:

\boxed{I(C; M_t) < R_C(\varepsilon) \;\Longrightarrow\; D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}(t) > \varepsilon}

but

\boxed{I(C; M_t) \ge R_C(\varepsilon) \;\not\Longrightarrow\; D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*}(t) \le \varepsilon.}

And for the biological endpoint:

\boxed{D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*} \le \varepsilon_C \;\not\Longrightarrow\; D_{\mathcal A}^{Y,*} \le \varepsilon_Y.}

L.2 The Information Ladder

\boxed{
\begin{array}{c}
\textbf{Level 1: Representation} \\
I(C; M_t) \\
\downarrow \\
\textbf{Level 2: Class Decoding} \\
D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*} \\
\downarrow \\
\textbf{Level 3: Regenerative Execution} \\
D_{\mathcal A}^{Y,*} \\
\downarrow \\
\textbf{Level 4: Regenerative Phenotype} \\
Y
\end{array}}

L.3 Central Thesis

\boxed{
\text{Task-specific sufficiency of regenerative positional memory is a property of }
(M_t, \Theta_{\mathcal A}, \varepsilon),
\text{ not of } M_t \text{ alone.}
}

L.4 Methodological Constraints

· No new mechanistic model. This work provides a measurement and constraint framework.
· Sufficiency is relational. M_t is not sufficient or necessary in itself.
· Temporal dynamics matter. I(C; M_t) is not static.
· Do not conflate X and C. Memory need not preserve exact position, only regenerative class.
· Do not conflate measured and biological memory. M_{\rm measured} \neq M_{\rm biological}.
· Do not treat the decoder as passive. Decoder-only write-back cannot increase I(C; M_t) without exogenous input.
· Do not rely on Fano alone. Rate-distortion thresholds are tighter and prior-aware.
· Do not claim that sufficient information guarantees regenerative success. The converse of the impossibility theorem does not hold.
· Do not claim that parameterization alone ensures estimability. Identifiable or functionally identifiable parameterization is required.
· Do not present MI estimates as exact. All estimators provide lower bounds or estimates.
· Do not overclaim causal identification. A change in I(C; M) and D_{\mathcal A}^{C,*} is consistent with a causal contribution but does not uniquely identify it without intervention-level identification.


